{"conjecture": "Software systems degrade because individual changes are validated against local syntax and tests, not against the global dependency grammar they implicitly construct. Each reviewed modification adds a latent edge to a directed graph of state transitions that is never fully traversed by unit tests, meaning the system's true behavior space grows superlinearly while its tested behavior space grows linearly. Thus, the system becomes harder to change not because any single change is wrong, but because the combinatorial state space of all past changes is never fully constrained by any individual review."}
